;;; A kernel dunder among the blind enumerator's objects —
;;; regression `closed-by-rule` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A table of ten digits that its own rule closes, `(__closed__ digit)`
;;; derived and never written as a fact, beside a relation the program
;;; declares and does not close, `total`. No file writes a fact headed by
;;; `__closed__`, and none writes the word in an argument position.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The program states no `(solve …)`, so its hypotheses are the facts of its
;;; open declared relation over its names: `(total 0)` to `(total 9)`, ten
;;; candidates. `__closed__` is the kernel's word for *this relation is
;;; closed*, not a thing the puzzle is about, and no program means it as an
;;; object. Nothing restricts the ten, so R is the whole cube, 1 024 states.
;;; It is what `dunder-object-closed-by-fact.ein` answers, the same program
;;; with its closure written as a fact.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Eleven candidates, `(total __closed__)` the eleventh, and R 2 048 states:
;;; the head of a fact only a rule derives is in the KB's names index and in
;;; no registry, so the blind enumerator took it for an object. It leaves the
;;; kernel's two dunders out by name now, and the answer is the one above.

(relation digit Digit :why "{?1} is a digit")
(digit 0) (digit 1) (digit 2) (digit 3) (digit 4)
(digit 5) (digit 6) (digit 7) (digit 8) (digit 9)
(rule digits-closed () :match () :assert (__closed__ digit)
  :why "the digits are all written: none is ever guessed")
(relation total Number :why "the total is {?1}")
(query :goal (total ?x))
